This is an ill-defined way to specify a flexible array, and going forward I want to avoid flexible arrays altogether for ergonomic reasons:

https://github.com/openbmc/libpldm/blob/9c57ef5d371cec85075c7524a34c77bb8d8d884b/docs/checklists/changes.md#L154-L173

Looking at the message, this might perhaps be best defined as:

```
struct pldm_get_event_receiver_resp {
    uint8_t completion_code;
    uint8_t transport_protocol_type;
    union {
        uint8_t mctp_eid;
        struct variable_field vendor_specific;
    } event_receiver_address;
};
```

So part of the trick of the `msgbuf` APIs is that they can handle errors in aggregate. We can reduce this to:

```c
pldm_msgbuf_insert(buf, event_receiver_info->completion_code);
pldm_msgbuf_insert(buf, event_receiver_info->transport_protocol_type);
pldm_msgbuf_insert(buf, event_receiver_info->event_receiver_address.mctp_eid);
return pldm_msgbuf_complete(buf);
```

So the `*_p()` APIs exist to deal with legacy public APIs that deconstruct message members into a bunch of pointer parameters to the decode function. We don't have that concern here as you're passing through a `struct pldm_get_event_receiver_resp *`.

You can change these to e.g the following, throughout:

```
pldm_msgbuf_extract(buf, resp->completion_code);
```

Note that in this specific case of extracting the completion code, if the completion code isn't success, then it's likely that there's no following content in the message. You can use `pldm_msg_has_error()` like so, prior to initialising the msgbuf:

```
rc = pldm_msg_has_error(msg, payload_length);
if (rc) {
	resp->completion_code = rc;
	return 0;
}

rc = pldm_msgbuf_init_errno(...)
```

We can't do this unless we know it is actually an MCTP EID that's in the message. You need to do this extraction conditionally:

```
rc = pldm_msgbuf_exract(buf, resp->transport_protocol_type);
if (rc) {
    return pldm_msgbuf_discard(buf, rc);
}
if (resp->transport_protocol_type == PLDM_TRANSPORT_PROTOCOL_TYPE_MCTP) {
    pldm_msgbuf_extract(buf, resp->event_receiver_addres.mctp_eid);
} else {
    return pldm_msgbuf_discard(buf, -ENOTSUP);
}

return pldm_msgbuf_complete_consumed(buf);
```

Please no casting like this - it's what's causing the problems you noted in the comment thread in the header

Rather, you've already extracted the value into `resp->transport_protocol_type`, so we can do:

```
if (resp->transport_protocol_type != PLDM_TRANSPORT_PROTOCOL_TYPE_MCTP) {
    return pldm_msgbuf_discard(buf, -ENOTOSUP);
```

We need to make sure the EID is the object in the union first. Otherwise this access may be invalid. Perhaps add the following after line 2537 above:

```
/* See Table 2, DSP0245 v1.4.0 */
if (event_receiver_info->transport_protocol_type != PLDM_TRANSPORT_PROTOCOL_TYPE_MCTP) {
    return -ENOTSUP;
}
```
